#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514} :activity/id #uuid "0c5859d1-4ff1-5efe-90ef-d19961e8bcc4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/id #uuid "ca183854-66cd-5b1f-9d29-03785c3216b8" :topic/math-academy-id 861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088889 :tutorial/content "In mathematics, rectangular arrays containing numbers are called **matrices**. \n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A$ below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis particular matrix has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. The numbers in the matrix are called **entries** or **elements.**\n\nThe number of rows and columns in a matrix does not need to be the same. For example, consider another matrix that has $3$ rows and $4$ columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 13 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe **dimensions** of the matrix are the number of rows and the number of columns. For example, the matrix $M$ shown above has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, we write the dimensions of $M$ as $3 \\times 4$ (read as \"three by four\")." :tutorial/id #uuid "d68f01b8-8bc6-59a1-8d80-d284abbcdf6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22697b73-625d-545a-ba00-223291b29933" :step/math-academy-id 16069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335982}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b1b7d3eb-f611-589c-99d3-92b8c28fd2c4" :step/math-academy-id 16070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348064}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "17d76850-618a-5b25-88fc-fc5296c2deb2" :step/math-academy-id 16073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "Some matrices have only one column, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **column vectors**.\n\nOther matrices have only one row, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $D$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **row vectors**.\n\n**Note:** Every number can be thought of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix. For instance, we can consider the number $5$ as the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\,5\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1628e679-983a-5be7-9af6-81f0f45fa29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2984 :tutorial/title "Matrices With One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "1f0e819b-30a9-5921-8910-450a2910edb7" :step/math-academy-id 16114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088898 :tutorial/content "A **square matrix** is a matrix where the number of rows is equal to the number of columns.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a square matrix because it has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Its dimensions are $2 \\times 2.$\n\nIn general, the dimensions of a square matrix are $n \\times n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06286713-0de9-5c18-959f-04de81bee510" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3115 :tutorial/title "Square Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "15b361ac-babf-50db-b55f-4a4ad993eb69" :step/math-academy-id 16855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403328}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} :step/id #uuid "b0393a9e-8102-5d2d-8b20-ac2709992291" :step/math-academy-id 18438 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088984 :tutorial/content "Matrices arise naturally in *many* real-world problems. Weather forecasting, image processing and computer graphics, video games, and online customization algorithms are just a few of the many applications of matrices. \n\nLet's consider an example where we can use a matrix to model bus routes in a city. \n\nThe diagram below shows the bus routes between three stations (Station A, Station B, and Station C) in a particular city, where $A, B,$ and $C$ represent the stations and the arrows represent the directed routes between them. \n\n![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nNote that the circles $A,B,$ and $C$ in our diagram are called **nodes**.\n\nWe wish to represent this same diagram using a matrix. Let's start by putting this information into a table like the one below:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** |  |  |  |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nLet's now fill in the rows of our table. We begin by considering all of the bus routes from Station A to the other stations:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}0$ bus routes from $A$ to itself.\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $C.$\n\nFilling in the first row of the table, we get the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\color{blue} 0$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nFilling in the remaining rows in the same way, we get:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, keeping only the essential information, we obtain the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "523d25fd-6b49-5cd7-ac05-80ac6fa0f3d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6114 :tutorial/title "Using Matrices for Modeling"} :step/id #uuid "f1c6aa85-8a73-5586-afeb-0d6c77ff0b9a" :step/math-academy-id 37144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299760}] :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"} :step/id #uuid "e1afb939-c40e-55de-8823-355f35294017" :step/math-academy-id 37441}] :activity/title "Introduction to Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928}]}
